   "type": "answer",
   "content": "Conclusion: the counters are consistent and there is no current memory pressure on esx-21. You do not need to move VMs off it.\n\nMechanism and reason\nESXi Memory State High means free memory is well above Soft/Hard thresholds, so no reclaim is happening now. Balloon 0 all day and Swap in/ Swap out / Compression / Decompression rates all 0 in every sample confirm no current reclaim.\n\nHost-level Swap Consumed 3.1 GB and Compressed 1.2 GB are past reclamation, not current pressure. On ESXi ballooned, compressed and swapped memory are what was reclaimed earlier, not what is under pressure now; one can be 0 while the others are not because reclaim order depends on memory state and per-VM settings. `vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/comparisons/host-memory-metrics`\nCompressed pages can stay compressed for a long time when nothing requests them. `vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/host/compressed`\n\nSwap Consumed is what sits in the swap file now. It is not Swap Out minus Swap In. Swap Out and Swap In are cumulative totals; a VM can arrive with memory already swapped via vMotion, or a limit can have caused swap in the past, and Swapped stays flat with zero rates thereafter. `vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/host/swap-used`; `vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/virtual-motherboard/swapped`\n\nESXi Memory Usage maps to Consumed and is a capacity reading; pressure shows in ballooning, compression, swapping and VM Memory Contention, not in Usage %. `vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/host/usage`\nESXi Memory Latency is a normalized average of VM Memory Latency and hides the VM that suffers; use the maximum VM Memory Contention across the host’s VMs instead. `vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/host/latency`\n\nWhat to check next\nPer-VM Swapped, Compressed, Ballooned, Balloon Target and VM Memory Latency / Memory Contention for the VMs on esx-21; whether any VM or resource pool has a memory limit that could have caused past swap/compression on a host with free memory; and confirm the sample interval and that the 3.1 GB swap / 1.2 GB compressed are historical leftovers with no swap-in/decompression activity.",
